From: GoProSlowYo <68455785+goproslowyo@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 21:25:11 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 15/21] dndcp: Start a native Wayland drag from the faked press

Turn the press on the overlay into a wl_data_device drag.
PointerButton() starts the drag from inside the handler of the press,
with its serial, because compositors refuse a wl_pointer.enter serial.
The source offers the armed formats with the copy action. There is no
drag icon, since the host draws one on its side of the window.
WaylandDrag_StartOnPress() reads the module's own connection until the
press arrives, for at most the caller's timeout.

The overlay must leave the drag once the grab exists. sway and KWin
honour an empty input region during a drag, but Mutter ignores it, so
the overlays are unmapped instead, which works on all three. Committing
a null buffer returns a layer surface to its initial state, so the
configure that follows must not attach the buffer again.

The module has to find out whether start_drag succeeded. A drag across
surfaces without a data device produces no events for as long as it
lasts, so a timeout on events would end a real drag. A wl_display.sync
follows start_drag instead. The compositor sends cancelled for a refused
drag before it answers the sync, so an answer while the source still
exists means the grab exists, and a watchdog covers a compositor that
answers nothing. The drag ends on cancelled or dnd_finished, and a
target that never sends dnd_finished after a drop is given 10 s. The
caller's done() runs exactly once, although some compositors send both
events.

A send writes the requested type to a non-blocking pipe and stops at a
deadline, and all the sends of one drag share a budget, so a target that
stops reading cannot stall the main loop. The module's own data device
events show when the overlay is offered the drag, which would mean the
overlay is taking the drop from the application under it, and it
destroys the offers they carry. WaylandDrag_Pump() handles what the
compositor has sent without waiting, and WaylandDrag_Cancel() ends a
drag at once by destroying the source.
